\label{ch:incremental}

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:incremental-intro}

Traditional compilers operate in a batch mode: the entire program is compiled from scratch each time. This model worked when programs were small and compilation was fast. But modern software projects consist of millions of lines of code, and even small changes can trigger lengthy recompilation. \emph{Incremental compilation} addresses this by reusing the results of previous compilations, recomputing only what has changed.

This chapter explores the application of incremental computation to compilation, from basic dependency tracking to sophisticated change propagation algorithms and the emerging paradigm of compilers as persistent services.

\section{Incremental Computation Applied to Compilation}\label{sec:inc-comp}

\subsection{The Problem}\label{sec:inc-problem}

\begin{definition}[Incremental Compilation]\label{def:incremental-comp}
\emph{Incremental compilation} is a compilation strategy in which a change to the source program triggers recompilation of only those parts of the program affected by the change, reusing the results of previous compilations for unaffected parts.
\end{definition}

The goal is to reduce the cost of recompilation from $O(|P|)$ (where $|P|$ is the program size) to $O(|\Delta|)$ (where $|\Delta|$ is the size of the change), or at least to something significantly smaller than $|P|$.

\subsection{Levels of Incrementality}\label{sec:inc-levels}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{File-level}: recompile only the files that changed. This is what \texttt{make} does.
  \item \item \textbf{Module-level}: recompile only the modules whose interfaces or implementations changed. This is what build systems like Bazel and Gradle do.
  \item \textbf{Function-level}: recompile only the functions affected by a change. This requires finer-grained dependency tracking.
  \item \textbf{Analysis-level}: recompute only the analysis results affected by a change. This is the domain of incremental dataflow analysis.
  \item \textbf{IR-level}: update only the IR nodes affected by a change. This is the most fine-grained approach.
\end{itemize}

\section{Dependency Tracking}\label{sec:dependency-tracking}

\subsection{Dependency Graphs}\label{sec:dep-graphs}

\begin{definition}[Compilation Dependency Graph]\label{def:dep-graph}
A \emph{compilation dependency graph} is a directed graph where nodes represent compilation artifacts (source files, modules, functions, analysis results) and edges represent dependencies (``$A$ depends on $B$'' means that a change to $B$ may require recomputing $A$).
\end{definition}

