\subsection{Fine-Grained Dependencies}\label{sec:fine-grained-deps}

Effective incremental compilation requires tracking dependencies at a fine granularity:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Definition--use chains}: which definitions does each use depend on?
  \item \textbf{Call graph edges}: which functions call which other functions?
  \item \textbf{Type dependencies}: which types does each function depend on?
  \item \textbf{Import dependencies}: which modules does each module import?
  \item \textbf{Analysis dependencies}: which analysis facts does each optimization decision depend on?
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Interface-Based Dependencies}\label{sec:interface-deps}

A key insight is that dependencies should be through \emph{interfaces}, not implementations:

\begin{definition}[Interface Dependency]\label{def:interface-dep}
Module $A$ depends on module $B$'s \emph{interface} (its exported types, functions, and constants), not its implementation. Changes to $B$'s implementation that do not affect its interface do not require recompiling $A$.
\end{definition}

This is the principle behind module systems like ML's, Haskell's, and Rust's, where the interface (signature, module header) is separate from the implementation.

\section{Change Propagation}\label{sec:change-propagation}

\subsection{The Propagation Algorithm}\label{sec:propagation-alg}

When a change occurs, the compiler must determine what to recompute:

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Incremental Change Propagation}
\label{alg:change-propagation}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Procedure{PropagateChange}{$\Delta$, $\text{dep\_graph}$}
  \Let{changed}{\text{nodes affected by } \Delta}
  \Let{worklist}{changed}
  \While{worklist is not empty}
    \Let{node}{\textsc{Dequeue}(worklist)}
    \Let{new\_value}{\textsc{Recompute}(node)}
    \If{$\text{new\_value} \neq \text{node.value}$}
      \State $\text{node.value} \gets \text{new\_value}$
      \For{each dependent $d$ of node}
        \State \textsc{Enqueue}(worklist, $d$)
      \EndFor
    \EndIf
  \EndWhile
\EndProcedure
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Self-Adjusting Computation}\label{sec:self-adjusting}

\begin{definition}[Self-Adjusting Computation]\label{def:self-adjusting}
\emph{Self-adjusting computation} is a framework (developed by Acar, Blelloch, and others) in which the computation records its dependency structure during execution and automatically re-executes only the affected parts when inputs change.
\end{definition}

The key data structures are:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Modifiable values}: values that can change over time.
  \item \textbf{Dependency graph}: records which computations depend on which modifiable values.
  \item \textbf{Change propagation}: when a modifiable value changes, re-executes only the computations that depend on it.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Adapting to Compilation}\label{sec:adapting-to-compilation}

Self-adjusting computation can be applied to compilation by treating source files as modifiable values and compilation phases as computations that depend on them. When a source file changes, only the compilation phases that depend on it are re-executed.

\section{Reactive Compilation}\label{sec:reactive}

\subsection{The Reactive Model}\label{sec:reactive-model}

\begin{definition}[Reactive Compilation]\label{def:reactive-comp}
\emph{Reactive compilation} treats the compilation process as a reactive system: source code changes are inputs, and compiled artifacts are outputs. The compiler maintains a dependency graph and automatically updates outputs when inputs change.
\end{definition}

This is analogous to reactive programming frameworks (like React, RxJS, or Svelte) where changes to inputs automatically propagate through a dependency graph to update outputs.

\subsection{Implementation}\label{sec:reactive-impl}

A reactive compiler maintains:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Cells}: cached computation results (e.g., the AST for a file, the IR for a function).
  \item \textbf{Dependencies}: which cells depend on which other cells.
  \item \textbf{Invalidation}: when a cell changes, its dependents are marked dirty.
  \item \textbf{Recomputation}: dirty cells are recomputed on demand (lazily) or eagerly.
\end{itemize}

\section{Compiler as a Service}\label{sec:compiler-service}

\subsection{Long-Lived Compiler Processes}\label{sec:long-lived}

Instead of starting a new compiler process for each compilation, the compiler runs as a long-lived service:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Persistent state}: the compiler maintains its internal state (parsed files, analysis results, IR) across compilations.
  \item \textbf{Incremental requests}: each compilation request specifies only what has changed.
  \item \textbf{Fast response}: because most state is cached, responses are fast.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Examples}\label{sec:service-examples}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Scala's Bloop}: a build server that maintains compilation state for Scala projects.
  \item \textbf{Kotlin's daemon}: a long-running process that caches compilation results.
  \item \textbf{Rust's rust-analyzer}: an LSP server that incrementally analyzes Rust code.
  \item \textbf{TypeScript's tsserver}: the TypeScript language server.
  \item \textbf{Dart's analysis server}: incrementally analyzes Dart code.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Language Server Protocol (LSP)}\label{sec:lsp}

The Language Server Protocol standardizes the interface between editors and language servers:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Text synchronization}: the editor sends file changes to the server.
  \item \textbf{Diagnostics}: the server sends errors, warnings, and hints.
  \item \textbf{Completion}: the server provides code completion suggestions.
  \item \textbf{Go to definition}: the server resolves symbol references.
  \item \textbf{Find references}: the server finds all uses of a symbol.
\end{itemize}

All of these require incremental analysis: the server must update its analysis results as the user types, without re-analyzing the entire project.

\section{IDE Integration}\label{sec:ide-integration}

\subsection{Incremental Parsing}\label{sec:incremental-parsing}

IDEs need to parse code as the user types. Incremental parsers update the parse tree in response to small changes:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Tree-sitter}: an incremental parsing library that produces a concrete syntax tree and updates it efficiently when the source changes.
  \item \textbf{rust-analyzer}: uses an incremental parser that re-parses only the changed regions.
  \item \textbf{Dart's analyzer}: uses a fully incremental parser and resolver.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Incremental Type Checking}\label{sec:incremental-type-checking}

Type checking can also be performed incrementally:
\begin{itemize}
  \item When a type annotation changes, only the functions that use that type need to be re-checked.
  \item When a function body changes, only the function and its callers (if the signature changed) need to be re-checked.
  \item Type inference results can be cached and invalidated based on dependency tracking.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Error Recovery and Partial Analysis}\label{sec:error-recovery}

During interactive editing, the code is often in an invalid state (syntax errors, type errors). The incremental compiler must:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Parse as much of the code as possible, even with errors.
  \item Perform analysis on the valid portions.
  \item Report errors without crashing or losing all analysis results.
\end{itemize}

\section{Advanced Dependency Tracking}\label{sec:advanced-dep-tracking}

\subsection{Dependency Granularity and Precision}\label{sec:dep-granularity}

The effectiveness of incremental compilation depends on the precision of dependency tracking. Over-approximating dependencies causes unnecessary recompilation; under-approximating causes missed updates and stale results.

\begin{definition}[Dependency Precision]\label{def:dep-precision}
A dependency tracking system is \emph{precise} if it tracks exactly those dependencies that can affect the result of a computation. It is \emph{sound} if it never misses a real dependency (but may track some spurious ones).
\end{definition}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Dependency tracking precision levels}
\label{tab:dep-precision}
\begin{tabular}{llll}
\hline
\textbf{Level} & \textbf{Granularity} & \textbf{Precision} & \textbf{Overhead} \\
\hline
File & Source files & Low & Low \\
Module & Module interfaces & Medium & Low \\
Symbol & Individual definitions & High & Medium \\
Expression & Individual expressions & Very high & High \\
Value & Individual values & Maximum & Very high \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Semantic Change Detection}\label{sec:semantic-change}

A key challenge is determining whether a change is \emph{semantic}---whether it affects the meaning of the program. Consider:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Whitespace changes}: do not affect semantics; no recompilation needed.
  \item \textbf{Comment changes}: do not affect semantics; no recompilation needed.
  \item \textbf{Variable renaming}: affects the name but not the semantics (if all references are updated).
  \item \textbf{Type annotation changes}: may or may not affect downstream type checking.
  \item \textbf{Function body changes}: affects the function and its callers (if inlined).
\end{itemize}

\begin{algorithm}[htbp]
\caption{Semantic Change Detection}
\label{alg:semantic-change}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Procedure{DetectSemanticChange}{$\text{old\_ast}, \text{new\_ast}$}
  \Let{diff}{\textsc{ComputeTreeDiff}(old\_ast, new\_ast)}
  \For{each change $c$ in diff}
    \If{$c$ is whitespace or comment}
      \State \textbf{continue} \Comment{Not semantic}
    \EndIf
    \If{$c$ is a rename with all references updated}
      \State \textsc{UpdateSymbolTable}(c)
      \State \textbf{continue} \Comment{Name change only}
    \EndIf
    \If{$c$ is a type signature change}
      \State \textsc{InvalidateDependents}(c.symbol, \text{type\_deps})
    \EndIf
    \If{$c$ is a body change}
      \State \textsc{InvalidateDependents}(c.symbol, \text{call\_deps})
    \EndIf
    \State \textsc{Recompute}(c.affected\_node)
  \EndFor
\EndProcedure
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Cross-Module Dependencies}\label{sec:cross-module-deps}

Dependencies often cross module boundaries. Tracking them requires understanding module interfaces:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Export dependencies}: if module $A$ exports type $T$, and module $B$ uses $T$, then $B$ depends on $A$'s export of $T$.
  \item \textbf{Implementation dependencies}: if module $A$ changes the implementation of function $f$ (without changing its signature), modules that call $f$ do not need recompilation.
  \item \textbf{Transitive dependencies}: if $A$ depends on $B$ and $B$ depends on $C$, then a change to $C$ may affect $A$ (if the change propagates through $B$'s interface).
\end{itemize}

