\subsection{Garbage Collection of Cached Results}\label{sec:cache-gc}

As the program evolves, cached query results accumulate. The system must garbage-collect results that are no longer needed:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Liveness analysis}: a cached result is live if it was used in the current revision.
  \item \textbf{Dependency-based GC}: a cached result can be freed if no live query depends on it.
  \item \textbf{Periodic sweeping}: periodically scan all cached results and free those not used since the last sweep.
\end{itemize}

\section{Parallel Incremental Compilation}\label{sec:parallel-incremental}

\subsection{Parallel Query Evaluation}\label{sec:parallel-queries}

Incremental compilation can be parallelized by evaluating independent queries concurrently:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Queries that depend on different inputs can be evaluated in parallel.
  \item When a query's dependencies are all satisfied, it can be evaluated immediately.
  \item A work-stealing scheduler can balance the load across threads.
\end{itemize}

\begin{definition}[Parallel Query Evaluation]\label{def:parallel-query}
\emph{Parallel query evaluation} evaluates independent queries concurrently, using a dependency graph to determine which queries can be evaluated in parallel and which must wait for their dependencies.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Handling Cycles in Parallel Evaluation}\label{sec:parallel-cycles}

If queries form a cycle (e.g., mutual recursion in type inference), parallel evaluation must detect and handle the cycle:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Cycle detection}: use a visited set to detect cycles during query evaluation.
  \item \textbf{Cycle recovery}: when a cycle is detected, use a fallback strategy (e.g., assume a default type, report an error).
  \item \textbf{Fixpoint iteration}: for some cyclic queries, iterate to a fixpoint (e.g., type inference with recursive types).
\end{itemize}

\section{Formal Foundations of Incremental Compilation}\label{sec:formal-foundations}

\subsection{Change Propagation Correctness}\label{sec:propagation-correctness}

The correctness of incremental compilation rests on a key property:

\begin{theorem}[Incremental Correctness]\label{thm:incremental-correct}
Let $C$ be a compilation function and $\Delta$ a change to the source program. If the incremental compilation algorithm produces result $R'$, then $R' = C(P + \Delta)$, where $P + \Delta$ is the modified program. That is, the incremental result is identical to a from-scratch compilation of the modified program.
\end{theorem}

Proving this theorem requires showing that:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item The dependency graph correctly captures all dependencies (soundness).
  \item The change propagation algorithm recomputes all affected results (completeness).
  \item The recomputation of each node produces the same result as a fresh computation.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Complexity of Incremental Compilation}\label{sec:inc-complexity}

\begin{definition}[Incremental Complexity]\label{def:inc-complexity}
The \emph{incremental complexity} of a compilation is the cost of propagating a change, measured as a function of the change size $|\Delta|$ and the dependency graph structure.
\end{definition}

In the best case, incremental compilation is $O(|\Delta|)$---only the directly affected nodes are recomputed. In the worst case (a change that cascades through the entire dependency graph), it is $O(|P|)$, no better than from-scratch compilation.

The average case depends on the program's dependency structure:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Modular programs}: changes tend to be local; incremental cost is low.
  \item \textbf{Monolithic programs}: changes may cascade widely; incremental cost is higher.
  \item \textbf{Interface-stable changes}: changes that do not affect interfaces have minimal incremental cost.
\end{itemize}

\section{Summary}\label{sec:incremental-summary}

Incremental and continuous compilation transforms the compiler from a batch process into a reactive service that maintains a live model of the program. By tracking dependencies at fine granularity and propagating only the effects of changes, incremental compilers can provide near-instant feedback even for very large projects. This is essential for modern IDE integration, where the compiler must respond to the user's edits in milliseconds.

\begin{historical}
Incremental compilation has been studied since the 1980s, with early work on incremental attribute evaluation and incremental dataflow analysis. The rise of large-scale software projects in the 1990s and 2000s made incremental compilation a practical necessity. Build systems like \texttt{make} (1976), \texttt{Jam} (1993), and modern systems like Bazel (2015) and Ninja (2011) implement file-level incrementality. The concept of self-adjusting computation (Acar et al., 2006) provided a theoretical foundation for fine-grained incrementality. The Language Server Protocol (Microsoft, 2016) standardized the interface between editors and language servers, driving the development of incremental compilers for languages like TypeScript, Rust, and Dart. The trend is toward fully incremental, reactive compilers that maintain a live, up-to-date model of the program at all times.
\end{historical}
